## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design scalable, policy-aware prompt orchestration with strong privacy guards and precise latency constraints, reflecting real-world multi-tenant systems at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy binding
- Guardrails to prevent system prompt leakage
- Modular routing to multiple tools
- Determinism testing under latency budgets
- Structured audit for compliance and debugging

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal routing prototype (conceptual)
function routePrompt(prompt, tenant, policyStore){
  const policy = policyStore[tenant];
  const guarded = applyGuard(prompt, policy);
  return { plan: ["SQL-builder","DataMasker","SummaryFormatter"], guarded };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate non-deterministic outputs across model variants under tight latency? 
- How would you scale tenant policies and audits across many tenants?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a modular, auditable routing layer for prompts with data-sensitivity and reproducibility concerns. It emphasizes concrete signals, edge-case handling, and a small prototype to demonstrate flow control.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt routing signals: intent, data_sensitivity, reproducibility
- Edge-case handling: ambiguity, missing sources, conflicting data
- Lightweight prototyping: minimal module map and payload schema

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(prompt) {
  const text = prompt.toLowerCase()
  const toLiterature = /(summarize|literature|research)/.test(text)
  const toExperiment = /(experiment|plan|propose)/.test(text)
  const needsAudit = /(audit|trace|reproducible)/.test(text)

  if (toLiterature && toExperiment && needsAudit) {
    return {
      modules: ['Literature-Summary', 'Experiment-Plan', 'Audit-Log-Formatter'],
      payload: { prompt, sources: [], trace: { confidence: 0.8 } }
    }
  }
  // Additional routing logic...
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend routing with governance constraints and to design testable prototypes.

## Key Concepts
- multi-module routing with governance
- signal design: intent, data_sensitivity, governance_policy
- edge-case handling: conflicts, missing role

## Code Example
```javascript
function route(prompt, signals){
  if (signals.governance_policy === 'restrict') return {module:'Data-Governance', payload:{prompt}};
  if (signals.data_sensitivity === 'PII') return {module:'PII-redactor', payload:{prompt}};
  return {module:'SQL-builder', payload:{prompt}};
}
module.exports = route;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you unit test the routing logic for edge cases like conflicting signals?
- How would you extend the router to support additional governance policies without breaking existing behavior?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to enforce data policies in prompt processing, not just theory. It probes edge-case handling and testability.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt safety layering
- Data_sensitivity tagging
- Policy-driven guard rails and auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal guard example
function guardPrompt(prompt, policy){
  const intent = parseIntent(prompt);
  const data = tagSensitivity(prompt, policy);
  if(!allowed(data, policy)){ throw new Error('Policy violation'); }
  const guarded = applyGuard(prompt, policy);
  const check = `compliant:${Boolean(guarded)}`;
  return {guardedPrompt: guarded, check};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model conflicting signals (privacy vs analytics) and resolve them?
- How would you test for prompt leakage across multi-hop prompts?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes system design for multi-tenant prompt governance with decomposition, guardrails, and auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt decomposition
- Tenant-specific policy enforcement
- Guardrail injection and leakage prevention
- Provenance and audit trails

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype illustrating flow
function governanceFlow(request, policy){
  const subs = decompose(request);
  const guarded = subs.map(s => injectGuard(s, policy));
  const finalOut = assemble(guarded);
  const audit = {tenant: policy.tenant, ts: Date.now(), steps: subs.length};
  return {finalOut, audit};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate guardrails against adversarial prompts and policy drift?
- What metrics would you instrument to measure governance efficacy and latency?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design scalable, privacy-aware prompt routing with safety checks under latency constraints, a common challenge in enterprise LLM deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-aware routing
- Privacy-preserving sanitization
- Latency-budget guided model selection
- Guardrails and veto logic
- Pluggable sanitizer architecture

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal gating prototype (JS)
class Gate {
  constructor() {}
  route(prompt, tenant) {
    const risk = {
      privacy: tenant.privacyRisk || 0,
      safety: tenant.safetyRisk || 0,
      latency: tenant.latencyBudget || 100
    };
    const model = risk.latency < 60 ? 'lite' : 'standard';
    const template = risk.privacy > 7 || risk.safety > 7 ? 'guarded' : 'default';
    return { tenant: tenant.id, model, template };
  }
}
const g = new Gate();
console.log(g.route('Prompt', {id: 'tenantA', privacyRisk:8, safetyRisk:5, latencyBudget:40}));
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test guardrails under adversarial prompts?
- How would you scale to thousands of tenants with dynamic policies?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical prompt versioning and drift handling with concrete routing decisions for enterprise data assistants.

## Key Concepts
- Drift detection via keyword overlap scoring
- Baseline prompt management with patch generation
- Three-tier routing: Intent-Detector, Safety-Guard, Response-Composer
- Signal tracking: intent, data_sensitivity, version_delta
- Edge case handling: renamed intents, missing baselines

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for drift detection and routing
function routePrompt(newPrompt, baselinePrompt) {
  const overlap = calculateKeywordOverlap(newPrompt, baselinePrompt);
  const hasSafetyIssues = detectSafetyKeywords(newPrompt);

  if (hasSafetyIssues) return 'Safety-Guard';
  if (overlap < 0.6) return 'Intent-Detector';
  return 'Response-Composer';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you calibrate the overlap threshold for different use cases?
- What approach would you use to store patches for audit trails?
- How would you handle version conflicts when multiple teams modify prompts simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design a practical, testable clarifier routing mechanism, not just theory.

## Key Concepts
- Rule-based routing and simple scoring
- Signals: intent_confidence, data_availability, user_role
- Edge-case handling: ambiguity, conflicts, missing data
- Payload design for downstream modules

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny prototype
function chooseClarifier(signals){
  const {intent_confidence, data_availability, user_role} = signals;
  if (intent_confidence < 0.5) return 'request-region';
  if (data_availability.includes('period')) return 'request-period';
  if (user_role==='analytics') return 'request-metric';
  return 'request-period';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this for multilingual prompts?
- How would you evaluate clarifier quality in automated tests?
